geographic_location :damarat do
  name "Damarat"
  summary "Damarat is a Lithren workshop excavation reached from Ithara, with a functioning ancient forming bed and a small camp whose work depends on it."
  subkind :region
  status :complete
  prominence :recognized
  playable_as :chronicle_location
  context_tags :outer_system, :surface, :cold, :archive, :yard
  tags :materials, :trade, :mystery, :danger, :subject_lithren
  descriptive_identity setting: "A pressure roof covers one terrace of an ancient works; curved offcuts line the warm passage and untouched terraces descend beneath frozen scree.", activity: "Workers make replacement panels while neighbors search for stolen setting bars and take sides over a buyer's offer to remove the bed.", hazards: "A thief has attacked an operator; armed guards seek entry to the camp, and old shafts and a surviving trap interrupt routes into the buried terraces."
  log "2026-09-23 — Gave the camp its own named operators and musician, restated the terrace evidence as findings, and rebuilt the theft and sale dispute around the injured operator."
  log "2026-09-23 — Renamed Joss Marran to Wyn Marran to separate them from Joss Orrin and Joss Keddis elsewhere in the corpus."
  prose <<~PROSE
    Damarat is the modern name of a workshop excavation below a long broken escarpment on #{ref :lithren, "Lithren"}. Its upper terrace holds a pressure roof, sleeping compartments and the #{ref :damarat_bed, "Damarat Bed"}. Other terraces continue beneath the scree. The exposed walls hold racks of curved bars and recesses whose spacing matches equipment removed before the present excavation.

    The #{ref :damarat_opening, "opening in 2433"} established that the bed could form supplied material when fitted with a modern drive. Crews now bring stock from #{ref :ithara, "Ithara"} and leave with panels, rounded containers and replacement covers. Ordinary manufacture pays some of the cost of examining the building around it.
  PROSE
  prose <<~PROSE, section: :people, heading: "The Occupied Terrace"
    #{ref :tamet, "Tamet"} helped repair the bed and returns to study it. The resident operators make ordinary repairs themselves now, and Brisa Kell, the oldest of them, runs the shift board from a stool by the drive housing. She takes commissions, teaches new hands by letting them ruin cheap stock, and owns a set of tools bought with her shares that she lends to each person exactly once. Materials investigators from the #{ref :ithara_comparative_expedition, "comparative expedition"} book time between those orders. They leave marked trial stock in the rack and sometimes return to find a household repair made with the setting they had thought too shallow to be useful.

    #{ref :ressa_dorr, "Ressa Dorr"} supplies the eating room. Its bowls are often warm from the final wash when a new shift arrives. Workers hang finished covers along the sleeping passage until a carrier takes them, so bunks open between shining convex faces. A visitor with a damaged case may find someone measuring it before they have finished eating.

    Visiting craft use the freight approach while residents take parties along a marked side shelf. #{ref :tovin, "Tovin"} brings #{ref :ulveth, "Ulveth"} here, and #{ref :osen, "Osen"} knows routes farther out than the camp's usual excursions. Residents meet arriving ships on their own account. A worker who refuses Tovin's hold may wait beside the meal table with a packed case until another captain offers a berth, or take a worse price to leave before the next shift.
  PROSE
  prose <<~PROSE, section: :geography, heading: "Beyond the Work Lights"
    The occupied floor bridges shafts once used by belts and counterweights. A lit inspection trench gives access beneath the bed; farther openings have temporary covers. Rows of shallow sockets continue into the next terrace in a spacing unlike the working machine's. The camp's map is a sheet pinned in the eating room, drawn in several hands, with each reachable edge marked and the terraces below trailing off the bottom of the paper.

    One closed passage contains a dropped slab with a tread and release linkage still connected. Its path and trigger can be inspected from a maintenance recess. It bars a route to further racks of forming bars. A brace could hold the slab high enough for a crawling person, but its release linkage continues into the wall beyond the visible catch. Another route crosses loose slope material outside the enclosure. People working on that approach can see the backs of the racks through a narrow break, including a curved bar too broad to bring through their present opening.
  PROSE
  prose <<~PROSE, section: :history, heading: "Earlier Work on the Terraces"
    Offcuts embedded beneath a resurfaced floor preserve shallow rounded forms made before the bed's present surrounding walkway. Above that floor lie heavier scrap strips and impressions where long braces were stacked. The building made different things at different stages, and the scraps give the order: rounded forms first, braces later. Selven's people have asked twice whether the braces were for walls. Brisa tells them a brace holds up whatever its customer wanted held up, and sells them offcuts to take home and argue over.

    A set of resting niches beside the older floor was partly closed when the walkway widened. One retained its low shelf and a polished hollow where a container had repeatedly stood. Food residue in that hollow differs from the mineral packing farther inside the works. People ate and rested beside production before later builders gave more space to moving loads.

    The cargo carriage #{ref :ivara, "Ivara"} adds one more event: selected pieces carried out through Sahel's breached fortification came to rest on the lower loading shelf. Where its arrival falls among the terraces' alterations is still being worked out. In 2435 Osen reached it by the exposed outer approach, since the interior passage stays shut behind the dropped slab. The carriage ties Damarat to one departure from the fort, and it gives his hoped-for crew a work worth repairing.
  PROSE

  prose <<~PROSE, section: :resources, heading: "The Cases Beside the Bed"
    A sample case in the occupied workshop contains living #{encyclopedia_ref :avreth} from a sealed recess below the terraces. Its small cones whistle beneath the case's circulating air. The keepers can hear a second colony through a nearby pressure door, with a lower note that stops whenever the workshop ventilation starts. Its chamber shares a cracked wall with the open cut. They have packed the crack from this side, leaving the door closed while they find a way to reach the wet growth with its pressure intact.

    Imra Tallo, a visiting musician, has raised a daughter colony in a case fitted with a foot bellows and a return tube. She is taking it to #{ref :veyr, "Veyr"}, where she intends to answer the stamping from its suspended boat. Water gathering at the cones' mouths changes the notes during a phrase. Here she plays after the late shift, repeating a rhythm until the workers start answering; when a heavy droplet falls, she catches the changed pitch with her next bellows stroke. The instrument case she brought here empty hangs beside the old sample box, its interior lined with fresh living threads.

    Makers test recovered #{encyclopedia_ref :thavren} beside the forming bed. A painted bowl on the shelf shows the colors left by an uneven firing. Several workers now keep exhausted pigment for household decoration instead of putting every old cake into the buyers' trays.
  PROSE
  prose <<~PROSE, section: :tensions, heading: "A Machine Worth Taking"
    Wyn Marran found fresh cutting oil on the empty rack where the finest setting bars had hung, followed the marks into the sleeping passage and woke on the floor with a split scalp. He runs the simpler settings on the next shift with his head wrapped. He says two people carried the case past the sleeping rooms before the blow, and the two grow taller each time he tells it at supper.

    The case vanished in the middle of a larger quarrel. #{ref :ilven_sarith, "Ilven Sarith"} has offered to buy the bed and lift it out, and the #{ref :damarat_removal, "dispute"} over his money has split the terrace down the sleeping passage. Operators who dug the terrace out want to be paid for the danger and leave. Brisa and the families who came with her have sunk their shares into tools that only earn while the bed stays. Each side suspects the other of the theft: the sellers say the stayers hid the bars to make the machine worth less, and the stayers say the buyer's people took the best parts early to lower the price.

    Armed guards hired for the proposed freight movement walk the approach every few days and have offered to search the rooms. The residents keep their doors shut to them. Two households have started sleeping in shifts beside their own lockers instead.
  PROSE
  gm_note :triggered_by, "Bringing a damaged container or panel gets it measured for a local replacement. An operator finds a close match among the old settings, but that bar also fits an unopened rack below the camp."
  gm_note :complicates, "A worker asks the visitors to keep watch while she follows an oil mark from the stripped setting rack. The marks end at a room belonging to someone who publicly opposed the sale."
end
relate :rel_damarat_part_lithren, :part_of, :damarat, :lithren
relate :rel_osen_at_damarat, :operates_in, :osen, :damarat, since: 2433
relate :rel_comparative_at_damarat, :operates_in, :ithara_comparative_expedition, :damarat, since: 2433
